San Jose��s official connection to Ireland stretches back to 1986, when former mayor Tom McEnery helped the city forge a sister city program with its capital, Dublin.
And to celebrate the 30th anniversary of that partnership, a reception recently was held at the Silicon Valley Capital Club.
But San Jose and the rest of the Bay Area��s original ties to Ireland can be traced back more than 100 years.
��The Irish American community is one of the oldest in California, it��s as old as the Spanish,�� said Tony Becker from AmeriCeltic, a nonprofit that promotes awareness, celebration and preservation of Celtic heritage. ��It��s no accident that Dublin, the capital of Ireland, has a sister city relationship with San Jose.��
More than 2.5 million Irish people immigrated to North America during the mid to late 1800s; many were motivated by a desire to own land and freely worship as Catholics, which was banned in their homeland.
��When they came to California they were able to be truly free, they were free to practice their religion and own land,�� Becker said. ��Both Spain and Mexico were Catholic and they allowed the Irish to practice their religion and encouraged it and allowed them to own land.��
Many early Irish immigrants acquired land grants from the government; some became major players who shaped the history of both the Bay Area and Ireland.
Becker said the California Gold Rush possibly played a role in the Irish independence movement, triggered when former San Francisco sheriff��s deputy Thomas Desmond traveled to Australia and freed Irish imprisoned by the British in 1876. Some believed Desmond was sent there by James Phelan, who ran the First National Bank in San Francisco at the time and helped finance the escape with gold acquired during the Gold Rush, according to Becker.
The escape restored the spirits of people who cared about Irish freedom and was the catalyst to a new independence that resonated with Irish Catholics on the West Coast.
��We think some of the money that bought the ship came from the bank because they sent Thomas Desmond,�� Becker said. ��He was probably carrying the money on him.��
At the time, Becker said, San Jose��s Irish population rivaled that of San Francisco��s.
��The percentage in the 1870s, 1880s up until the 1920s, the majority of Irish in San Jose was exactly the same as it was in San Francisco,�� he said. ��We had just as many Irish here relative to the other populations.�� And Santa Clara County was one of the most important counties for the Irish in California up until the early 1900s, he added.
Descendants of San Jose��s Irish settlers continued to play a major role in Ireland��s economic development, thanks to the founding of the San Jose-Dublin Sister City program.
McEnery ��wanted to promote Irish engineers in the high-tech industries of Silicon Valley,�� said San Jose-Dublin board vice chairman Tim Quigley. The interest was mutual; Dublin��s lord-mayor at the time wanted Ireland to become a main high-tech player in Europe and expand beyond its heavily agricultural economy.
