CLEVELAND, Ohio - Penn State is back with its colossal crowds, all white-clad, row upon row of them. They were almost 111,000 strong Saturday night, roaring in the dark, as Michigan went down, 42-13.
It is called a "White Out." It is staged each year for the biggest night game at home. It is a sonic avalanche of school spirit, disrupting the opposition's signals and putting the heart in Mount Nittany's Lions.
Whatever it looks like, however, it is no symbol of purity. Not at Penn State.
Showdown this Saturday
At least Ohio State will not have to play Penn State on Saturday in Beaver Stadium, the hall of the old mountain king, Joe Paterno.
There, in the grotesquely misnamed Happy Valley, isolation bred secrecy; insularity created Paterno's cult of personality; and perverted priorities protected the football program's sexual predator, former top aide Jerry Sandusky, but not the dozens of boys he raped.
The Big Ten's biggest game of the year will be instead played "on the banks of the Olentangy." You can almost hear the folksy voice of the now retired, 89-year-old Keith Jackson saying it.
The hypocrite's handbook
Paterno has been dead for five years. What he did or did not do in the child molestation scandal was, in either case, not close to enough and amounted to an abdication of moral responsibility.
The often championed slogans of that era could be a hypocrite's handbook -- "The Grand Experiment" and "Success with Honor."
The White Out, simply put, is not the best look for a school haunted by ghosts.
It has the trappings of a screwball cult, with all the Paterno true believers, the Joebots (Warning: Adult language), all waiting for the Mothership to fly them off to Cloud Cuckoo Land.
No one should forget the power concentrated in Paterno, who made underlings of his nominal bosses.
Nor should anyone overlook the continuing, stubborn denial of his guilt.
The White, the Red and the Black
On White Out nights, the players' helmets vibrate, almost like so many tuning forks, and play calls from two feet away are engulfed in the din, and the very ground seems to shake.
It is not like that at Ohio State, which has held a "Red Out" and a "Black Out" for the Penn State game at night in recent years.
Attendance is close to that at Penn State. But the noise has more places to go at the Horseshoe than in Beaver Stadium's enclosed bowl, and Columbus offers more things to do than State College.
The Ohio State fans also expect the other team's head on a platter, except in nervous, fretful times when they don't, as at almost any home Michigan game during John Cooper's coaching tenure.
The ground does not shake when trodden by the home team at the Shoe, although legends have walked there.
No death penalty
It is fitting that song "Zombie Nation" starts White Out games. If any program ever deserved the NCAA death penalty, it was Penn State.
But the Nittany Lions are the triumph of the undead. They are defending Big Ten champions, ranked second in the nation. Saturday's winner has the inside track to the College Football Playoff.
The NCAA could not shut down Penn State football for the horrific scandal and its cover-up because the organization's jurisdiction extends only over eligibility (academics) and competitive parity (illegal benefits), not over criminal cases.
Comparisons in crime and cynicism
The cynical conclusion is that, like North Carolina basketball and its bogus African-American studies case, like the big banks and Wall Street in 2008, Penn State football is{g} a{g} "{g}white{r}-{r}collar{g}"{g} program{g}.{g}
