When asked privately to assess prospects for their own businesses this holiday season, Chicago-area retailers admit serious concern, with most saying they expect profits to slip as shoppers spend less than they did last year.
The candid assessments are detailed in an annual survey by the Chicago office of Deloitte & Touche, a New York-based public accounting firm, of Chicago-area retailers. About 100 companies responded to the survey.
Overall, 71 percent of the retailers who responded said they expect the economy to worsen, with 81 percent saying the country already is in a recession.
Two-thirds of the businesses said they expect consumers to spend less on holiday giving than last year, with 29 percent saying shoppers will spend the same and 4 percent saying shoppers will spend more.
More than twice as many retailers than last year, or 37 percent compared with 15 percent, expect overall industry sales to fall below last year`s levels.
When asked about their own sales prospects, about half expressed some optimism, saying they believed their sales would grow between 0 and 10 percent. About a quarter of the respondents, however, said their own sales would be flat, and almost as many, 21 percent, said their sales would fall by as much as 10 percent. Only 4 percent said they believed sales would rise by 11 percent or more.
Shoppers looking for bargains probably will find sales starting a week or so earlier than last year, by early December, said Jerry Cohen, director of the Chicago retail unit for Deloitte & Touche.
Even though most firms have kept inventories trim with hopes of avoiding last year`s end-of-season markdowns, demand has slowed enough that many companies will need to begin cutting prices.
''I think retailers will react quicker to slowing demand,'' Cohen said.
Last year, widespread markdowns on Christmas merchandise hurt profits for many companies. Respondents this year said they believed profits will suffer again-but this time because of slow sales.
Almost three-quarters of the respondents said they expect industry profits overall will fall for the year. When asked about their own companies` profitability, more than half expected profits to be flat or lower than last year, while 45 percent expected higher profits, most in the 0 to 10 percent range.
