Turkey was warned yesterday that failure to help achieve a peace settlement in Cyprus would damage its chances of joining the European Union.
The European commission and member states ignored intensive last-minute lobbying by Ankara and said bluntly that Turkish membership could face "a serious obstacle" if Cyprus was not reunited before May.
Turkey is due to be told by the EU by next December whether it has met the criteria to qualify to start negotiations. Some observers doubt whether the overwhelmingly Muslim country will ever join the EU.
The warning came as Brussels told all 10 countries taking part in next May's "big bang" - Europe's fifth and most ambitious enlargement - to try harder to meet the conditions. But there was no suggestion that membership would be delayed. Poland, the largest country due to join, was the worst prepared.
All 10 have signed accession treaties and are participating informally at all levels of EU decision making. Their entry will take the EU to 25 countries and raise its population to 450 million. All newcomers are required to adopt the euro.
"It is my firm belief that the new member states will be able to solve the problems," said Günter Verheugen, the EU's enlargement commissioner. "This is the best prepared enlargement ever."
Poland was criticised for slow progress in nine important issues, including the free movement of persons, agriculture, food hygiene and fisheries.
But the others, Hungary, Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia, Lithuania, Latvia, Estonia, Cyprus and Malta were also told to take "immediate and decisive action" in 39 different sectors, ranging from farm inventories to public administration.
New members failing to complete preparations could be punished by a ban on their produce being sold in the single market. They could also be denied subsidies.
There are however, big variations: Slovenia, the richest of the eight former communist entrants, got the best report. Overall, there was a "good level of alignment" with EU laws, Mr Verheugen said.
This was the last regular monitoring report in preparation for enlargement, after which, like the current member states, the new entrants will probably freely break the rules.
The warning to Turkey increased pressure on Ankara to stop treating Cyprus as a security problem and use its leverage over the Turkish Cypriots.
Abdullah Gul, the foreign minister, responded immediately by insisting in a statement that Turkey would make a "great effort" to resolve the issue.
The commission believes conditions are favourable for a Turkish-Greek deal by next May, though UN-brokered talks collapsed earlier this year when Rauf Denktash, the Turkish Cypriot leader, rejected the peace plan.
Mr Verheugen said the warning to Turkey was not a new condition but a "factual statement", that without a settlement Ankara would have difficulty getting a positive decision on starting talks.
Turkey was praised for its reforms but was told to improve minority language rights and end torture.
Another candidate country, Bulgaria, was praised for making progress but Romania fell short of being given the "functioning market economy" status it craves.
Officials in Brussels said the biggest problems were in Poland, which is being cast as the EU's new bad boy because of its uncompromising demands on the new constitution.
The new members stand to reap huge economic benefits, but their integration will strain the EU's institutions and its €100bn (£68.4bn) annual budget.
Romano Prodi, the commission president, predicted that membership would reach 30 within a few years.
"This enlargement is unlike those that preceded it," said the commission report.
"Never before have so many accessions taken place at the same time. Never before have they been so thoroughly prepared, with a sweeping transformation of the economies and societies of the applicant countries."
