The $2.8 billion loss Qantas reported on Thursday is not as shocking as that figure implies.
But the numbers in the airline's annual results nonetheless make for unpleasant reading. They also provoke further questions about the company's long-term strategy and viability. Questions are already being asked by the airline's investors – and in Canberra – about whether Qantas' management and directors are best placed to continue to lead the organisation.
SHARE
Share on Facebook SHARE
Share on Twitter TWEET
Link Alan Joyce has made himself the public face of Qantas' travails. Photo: Daniel Munoz
This is an issue for the whole country.
It is an issue not only because of Qantas's position within the nation's history and psyche.
SHARE
Share on Facebook SHARE
Share on Twitter TWEET
Link Seeing red: Qantas is an issue for the whole country. Photo: Glenn Hunt
But it is also an issue because of the way in which Qantas appeals to that history, and the certainty that it will continue to do so, in arguing for a better deal within its regulatory environment.
Chief executive Alan Joyce will soon begin his seventh year at the head of Qantas.
Advertisement
The result he has just handed down was made to appear more painful than it may have by a $2.6 billion re-evaluation of aircraft purchased when the dollar was higher.
But to look a little deeper into the figure that Qantas wants investors to look at – the $646 million pre-tax underlying loss – is to see widespread evidence of self-inflicted financial trauma, notwithstanding the fact the share price rose in the wake of the result being announced.
The results, for instance, of the airline's decision to continue to pile on domestic flights in a capacity war with arch-rival Virgin are clear.
Qantas' domestic arm reported earnings before interest and tax of $30 million last year – more than $330 million less than it did the year before. This was partly the result, the airline conceded, of "market capacity increases ahead of demand".
In other words, domestic travellers got a great deal at the expense of the airline's profitability.
There is a similar story within Jetstar.
Qantas' lower-cost offshoot reported an earnings loss of $116 million, about $250 million worse than the year before. Mr Joyce partly blamed fuel costs. But again, much of the result could be attributed to the capacity wars he so willingly signed up to.
It is difficult, meanwhile, to see where the airline can turn things around internationally. The half-billion dollar earnings loss in Qantas's international division is about par these days, tie-ups with Emirates notwithstanding.
Apart from continuing cost savings from the remaining of 5000 staff redundancies already announced, and a more efficient use of its fleet, Qantas seems without a clear plan to arrest the decline in its international operations.
The revaluation of fleet should help, potentially making the foreign division more attractive to overseas investors in a rearranged corporate structure.
And the decision to retain the airline's frequent flyer business – which earned $286 million before interest and tax last year – rather than selling it off also suggests some level of confidence at board level.
That business, the board seems to have decided, makes most sense when combined with a well-functioning airline.
And it appears that airline will continue to be led by Mr Joyce, who has retained the support of his chairman, Leigh Clifford, since winning the job in late 2008.
After the drastic decisions he has taken as head of Qantas – most notably grounding the fleet in an industrial battle in late 2011 – Mr Joyce has made himself the public face of the airline's travails.
